*OUTPUT.DA</7 - - - Optional
*$
3. BINDING KINETICS
 Use KINRK(MATD06,RUNGK) FOR RUNGE-KUTTA 2ND ORDER BINDING:
 H + X1 === X2 === X3
 KINIP.DA CONTAINS STARTING PARAMETERS
4. SIMULATION FOR ANOTHER DAY


1. INSULIN BINDING:
   the program is INSBND.F4 (MATD15).  fits binding isotherm with 1 or
2 binding constants.  uses the theory of C. deHaen for the term A
which is the fraction of counts non-specifically packed into the 
pellet.
   As of this date I just fixed the error extimates for the parameters.
The formula is
	sig(p) = sqrt[A(i,i)]*wsig
sig(p) is the error estimate for parameter p, A(i,i) is the diagonal
 element of the inverse matrix used to determine the parameter updates.
wsig is a weighted deviation:
	wsig**2 = sum[ w(i)*dev(i)**2 ] / (npoint - nparam)
This formula is used because the weights are incorporated into A(i,i)
as the reciprocals, so inclusion of the weights in wsig removes this 
bias.
   INSMLE.F4:  this program was written to obtain the maximum likeli-
hood estimates of the parameters.  the input is the same as for INSBND
but the weights differ.  After the program finds the maximum likelihood
parameters, it tests whether the fractional deviations are normally
distributed.  If they are not, I have been doing the following:  delete
the points with extreme deviations until the fractional deviations are
gaussian (normally) distributed as calculated from chi - square.
   This program also calculates the Akaiki Information Content (AIC)
measure of the goodness of fit to a model.  If the data fits both
one site and two site models, the one with the minimum AIC is chosen
as long as the difference is greater the 2.  If the difference in AIC
is less than 2, choose the model with the smaller number of parameters.
[D.C.T. 15-APR-83]. INSMLE.F4(MATD15,AGAUSS,PCHISQ,GAMMA,FACTOR)
   PLTINS.F4 (AXIS,RSCALE,PLOTX,PLOTL,PLOTSY,MOVE) can be used to
construct a BJERRUM plot of the data, by writind an output file with
an INSBND option.  There is currently a PLTINS.BI file to compile and 
run this plotting program.

2. DECAY KINETICS (insulin release by dilution with cold):
   The program for 2 exponential decays with background is EXPDK.F4
(MATD15).  This program requires the parameter estimate file on unit 6.
When 2 exponential decays are fitted it is necessary to back - compute
the actual rate constants from the parameters.  The program GETK.F4
is used for this back - calculation.  Error estimates of the parameters
are output and also proppagated in GETK but this propagation part of
the program has not yet (30-mar-83) been checked.
   20-APR-83:
    The program EXPFD.F4 uses finite difference derivatives.  Needs
EDK.F4,MATD15.F4,AGAUSS.F4,PCHISQ.F4,GAMMA.F4,FACTOR.F4.
This program tests the normality of the deviations from the fitted
curve to enable one to verify that the model is proper, and the least-
square estimate of parameters is the MLE estimate as well.  Because a
histographic fit of the normal curve is given as output, one can also
delete the few points which may be outliers prohibiting the normal
error distribution to be the proper distribution of error.

3. BINDING KINETICS (insulin binding - pseudo-first order):
	      K12        K23
	X(1) ===== X(2) ===== X(3)
	      K21        K32
   several programs are available here.  simple binding to state X(2)
only is done with KINET.F4.  This model was found inadequate.  The pro-
gram is a modified version of EXPFIT.F4.
   The program KINETX.F4 uses explicit solution by the equations of
Frost and Pearson.  C. deHaen made all of the necessary derivatives
for solution of both the amplitudes and rate constants.  It did
not converge for the amplitudes, however, but this could be fond by
sweeping thru with specified amplitude.
   The program KINFD.F4 (KINSUB.F4) uses finite difference derivatives
for the parameters.  This program actually worked better than KINETX
in that the answers were very close to theose of the latter program and
also the amplitudes were also found.  As this proghram is easily modi-
fied for other models, it shold be valuable for future work also.

4. SIMULATION:
   The program KINSIM.F4 (RUNGSB) was designed for simulation of the
binding kinetics.  The reason for this was to examine the effect of the
pseudo-first order assumption in the binding kinetics and also to sim-
ulate data for binding curve setup.
